{"op":"add","path":"/elements/step2-corr-table","value":{"type":"DataTable","props":{"columns":[{"key":"predictor","label":"Predictor"},{"key":"pearson_r","label":"Pearson r"},{"key":"r_squared","label":"R²"},{"key":"spearman_rho","label":"Spearman ρ"}],"rows":[{"predictor":"customers (volume)","pearson_r":-0.23,"r_squared":0.053,"spearman_rho":-0.242},{"predictor":"arpu (health)","pearson_r":0.829,"r_squared":0.688,"spearman_rho":0.872}],"caption":"Correlation comparison: customer count vs. ARPU as predictors of churn MRR","highlight_max":true},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/step2-insight","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"Step 1 flagged the Enterprise plan as the top churn contributor and raised the volume-vs-health hypothesis; Step 2 resolves it: arpu is the dominant driver (Pearson r = -0.23 for customer count vs. 0.829 for ARPU, across 36 observations). churn_mrr correlates more strongly with ARPU (health) (r=0.829) than customer count (r=0.23). Customer count explains 5.3% of churn variance vs 68.8% for ARPU. (Steps 1, 2).","variant":"insight"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/elements/conclusion","value":{"type":"TextBlock","props":{"content":"Direct retention investment toward the Enterprise plan, where the churn MRR impact is greatest. Because arpu is the dominant predictor, the highest-value next step is a cohort-level breakdown of churned vs. retained customers within that plan — isolating whether the mechanism is declining headcount, compressing per-customer revenue, or both. Tracking ARPU alongside churn MRR monthly will surface early warning signals before they compound into larger revenue losses.","variant":"body"},"children":[]}}
{"op":"add","path":"/state/__synthesis","value":{"summary":"The Enterprise plan leads all segments with 9650 in average monthly churn MRR, making it the primary area of revenue risk (Step 1). Across 36 plan-month observations, arpu emerges as the stronger predictor of churn MRR — churn_mrr correlates more strongly with ARPU (health) (r=0.829) than customer count (r=0.23). Customer count explains 5.3% of churn variance vs 68.8% for ARPU. — confirming this is not a simple headcount problem (Step 2). Together the findings point to a concentrated, value-driven churn problem in a specific plan tier.","conclusion":"Direct retention investment toward the Enterprise plan, where the churn MRR impact is greatest. Because arpu is the dominant predictor, the highest-value next step is a cohort-level breakdown of churned vs. retained customers within that plan — isolating whether the mechanism is declining headcount, compressing per-customer revenue, or both. Tracking ARPU alongside churn MRR monthly will surface early warning signals before they compound into larger revenue losses."}}
